> I have a mouse problem, and nothing I do (poison traps, glue traps, spring traps) seems to help. I think I want to try getting a cat. But I don't want to go to a breeder. How should I adopt a cat with mouse catching in mind?
**assistant**:
> Adopting a cat to help with your mouse problem is a great idea, and there are several ways you can find a feline that might excel at being a mouser:
> 
> 1. **Visit Local Shelters and Rescues**: Start by visiting animal shelters and rescue organizations. There are often cats available that are in need of a home, and staff can guide you to those known for their hunting skills.
> 
> 2. **Barn Cat Programs**: Some shelters have barn cat programs catering to cats that are not suited for indoor living but are excellent natural hunters. These cats typically require less human interaction but can keep your property rodent-free.
> 
> 3. **Speak to Shelter Staff**: When visiting a shelter, explain your situation to the staff. They can recommend cats that have shown predatory behavior or alert you to any new arrivals with suitable temperaments.
> 
> 4. **Consider a Young Adult Cat**: Young adult cats (1-3 years old) are often more playful and have sharper hunting instincts, making them well-suited for catching mice than very young kittens or older cats.
> 
> 5. **Observe Cat Behavior**: If possible, observe cats in the shelter for signs of alertness and agility. Cats that are curious, quick, and play vigorously often have good hunting instincts.
> 
> 6. **Ask About Outdoor History**: Cats that have lived outdoors or in semi-feral conditions may have developed better hunting skills compared to cats that were exclusively indoor pets.
> 
> 7. **Adopt in Pairs**: If you have the resources and space, consider adopting two cats. Cats can work well together and form partnerships in hunting, which might enhance their effectiveness.
> 
> Make sure any cat you adopt is spayed or neutered, vaccinated, and microchipped, and provide them with proper care. A well-kept, happy cat is more likely to be an effective mouser. Good luck with your search!
